  // Chapter 41, Tropical Crops
  "ch41-img-03.jpg":
    "Infographic: preserving superior papaya plants through clonal propagation, to multiply a high-performing plant with the same genetic characteristics. Five steps. One, select the mother plant: a healthy, vigorous, productive plant with good fruit quality, observed for a complete fruiting season, free from disease and of the desired sex type, for example hermaphrodite. Two, induce side shoots: remove the growing tip to break apical dominance, so lateral buds grow into side shoots large enough for cuttings. Three, take and prepare cuttings: healthy, firm shoots with several nodes, lower leaves removed, cut cleanly just below a node. Four, root in the nursery: plant the cuttings in a clean, loose, airy medium under high humidity, moderate temperature and indirect light, moist but never waterlogged; roots usually develop in a few weeks. Five, harden and transplant: reduce humidity gradually, transplant to permanent PQNK beds and manage with mulch and SMM. Grafting, another practical method: grow a healthy papaya rootstock from seed, prepare a scion from the selected mother plant, join and tape them, and keep the plant in a shaded nursery until the graft heals. Tissue culture, for large-scale propagation: shoot tips or buds of the selected plant are grown in a laboratory under sterile conditions, giving many uniform plants that are acclimatised in a nursery. A PQNK papaya mother plant nursery: select 5 to 10 exceptional plants and keep them under PQNK, propagate by cuttings or grafting, grow the new plants on permanent raised beds under thick mulch, water only by SMM, never above a 4-inch furrow head, and multiply the best clones for future plantations. Important points: clones are genetically identical to the selected plant, cloning preserves a hybrid's characteristics, papaya grafts with high success, tissue culture gives large numbers of uniform plants, and all clones still need good PQNK management.",
  "ch41-img-04.jpg":
    "Infographic: papaya air layering, producing new plants directly on the mother plant, to preserve superior genetics from mature plants that no longer fruit. Twelve steps. One, select a healthy, vigorous mother plant that has produced high-quality fruit but stopped fruiting; never one that stopped because of disease or severe decline. Two, select a healthy lateral shoot about 1 to 2 cm across, or remove the growing tip to make new shoots on a plant kept for propagation. Three, choose a straight section 15 to 25 cm below the growing tip, with healthy leaves above it. Four, make a ring cut: remove a narrow ring of outer tissue about 1 cm wide and the exposed phloem, without cutting into the water-conducting tissue. Five, apply clean, loose, airy cocopeat or sphagnum moss, damp but not dripping, around the whole wound. Six, wrap it in clear plastic film tied at both ends and shade it with a loose, light-coloured cover. Seven, check weekly for moisture, decay and roots, adding a little water if dry, moist but never waterlogged; rooting may take several weeks or longer. Eight, do not separate early: wait until many healthy roots fill the medium. Nine, cut below the rooted section with a clean tool, keeping the medium intact. Ten, establish it in the nursery in a clean, loose, airy medium under moderate shade. Eleven, transplant to a permanent PQNK raised bed with thick mulch, irrigated by Soil Moisture Management, never above a 4-inch furrow water head. Twelve, grow on under PQNK: manage like any PQNK papaya with mulch and SMM; the plant carries the mother plant's fruit qualities into the field. Key points: use previously productive, non-fruiting plants; it keeps the characteristics of superior plants, including F1 hybrids; a practical method for farmers without a laboratory; fewer plants per mother plant than tissue culture; new plants go onto permanent PQNK raised beds under thick mulch. Not suitable: diseased plants, severely declined plants and plants with root damage. Result: a new plant with the same genetics as the selected mother plant, established under PQNK.",
  "ch41-img-05.jpg":
    "Infographic: PQNK banana, restoring a traditional crop, in Sindh, Pakistan's banana heartland, with a field report from the Nainital foothills of India. Sindh under conventional management: rising cost of production, falling fruit quality, diseased plants, and fertiliser, pesticide and fungicide. Photos show healthy plants, living cover of grass and old leaves, and strong roots. How PQNK does it: hardpan broken once, permanent raised beds, grass and mulch kept so the soil is never bare, old leaves laid down as mulch, no tillage, and no chemical fertiliser, pesticide or fungicide. Sindh under PQNK: lower cost of production, higher fruit quality, far less water and fertiliser, and disease-free plants. Also grown on PQNK in the frost-risk foothills of Nainital, India. The conventional view: more inputs for a declining crop. The PQNK reality: restore the soil, restore the crop. PQNK does not grow crops. PQNK grows soil that grows crops.",
  // KP-181, PQNK Response to the National Agricultural Biotechnology Policy 2025
  "kp181-img-01.jpg":
    "Infographic: the economic case for change is overwhelming. Two futures, one choice. Continuing conventional (ACI) agriculture costs the world about 1.9 trillion dollars a year in externalities paid by all of us: soil degradation 0.40 trillion, water depletion and pollution 0.35 trillion, pesticide and fertiliser runoff 0.30 trillion, biodiversity and pollinator loss 0.25 trillion, greenhouse gas emissions from tillage and inputs 0.30 trillion, and rural health damages 0.30 trillion. Adopting PQNK at scale gives an annual dividend of about 4.7 trillion dollars in saved externalities, from regenerated soils, restored aquifers and clean water, reduced inputs and pollution, thriving biodiversity and pollination, lower emissions and climate stability, and healthier rural communities, with a further dividend from better productivity, nutrition and ecosystem services, 7.5 trillion dollars in all. The net difference is 6.6 trillion dollars a year, larger than most national economies. The choice is not just ethical; it is economically irresistible: stop paying for destruction, start investing in regeneration.",
