  "kp173-img-01.jpg":
    "Four photographs of PQNK wheat plants lifted from the field with their roots. In each, a dense, fibrous, much-branched root system holds a large clump of dark, crumbly soil that clings to the roots, the sign of living soil biology.",
  "kp109-img-01.jpg":
    "Infographic: seed selection is a PQNK decision, comparing GMO, hybrid, seedless and open-pollinated seed, because both seed and soil biology decide what a crop can become. Open-pollinated, farmer-selected seed is nature's way: selection continues in the field every season, the population adapts to real pests, diseases, rainfall and soil, saves cost and keeps diversity and the farmer's sovereignty. GMO seed inserts genes across species boundaries, a laboratory decision with unknown long-term effects and corporate control. Hybrid F1 seed fixes one generation's genetics, so the farmer must buy the same cross every season, with no ongoing adaptation and narrowing genetics. Seedless varieties have reproduction removed by design: a genetic dead end and permanent dependency. The Green Revolution raised yields by breeding varieties that could absorb heavy fertiliser, at the cost of soil and genetic diversity. The PQNK solution restores the soil biology and lets open-pollinated seed express its true potential, with the farmer saving and replanting seed from the best plants in his own field.",
  "kp104-img-01-v11.jpg":
    "Infographic: converting a field to PQNK is a one-time engineering project, not a change of inputs but a complete redesign of the field for a permanent, no-till, biological system. The old canal-era layout of irregular small fields, uneven surfaces and flood irrigation, made for manual labour and animal power, gives way to long, consolidated, laser-levelled fields with permanent raised beds. The steps, done once: 1, topography survey; 2, redesign and consolidation of small plots into the longest fields possible; 3, hardpan breaking, before and after bulk earthmoving; 4, laser-precision levelling to a single uniform grade; 5, shaping permanent raised beds, typically a 42-inch bed top with an 18-inch furrow for traffic and water; 6, precision no-till planting directly through living or mulched cover; 7, applying PQNK biological principles forever. The result: no future re-levelling, re-shaping or tillage, and a farm ready for mechanised, climate-resilient, high-yield PQNK farming. Engineer the field once, apply biology forever.",
