  // KP-211, Why PQNK Sugarcane Does Not Need Earthing Up (main infographic)
  "kp211-img-01.jpg":
    "Infographic comparing conventional and PQNK sugarcane. Left, conventional sugarcane, planted in furrows, earthing up needed: sets are placed at the bottom of the furrow on compacted soil; a hardpan, or plough pan, stops deep root growth, so roots spread sideways in the upper layer; soil from the ridges is moved to the base of the plants, usually twice in the first four months, so the original furrow becomes a ridge and the ridge becomes a furrow; roots stay shallow and the tall, heavy crop is prone to lodging even after earthing up; the crown and new tillers are buried deeply where aeration is poorer; and at harvest about 12 inches of the sugar-rich basal cane often remains in the field. Right, PQNK sugarcane, planted on permanent beds, no earthing up: the hardpan is broken once with a subsoiler at about 22 inches before the beds are formed; sets are placed on the bed surface; roots travel deep into the soil profile and anchor the plant naturally; the soil is not disturbed again, so the crown stays near the aerated surface and tillering is healthy.",
  // Knowledge Papers, book-style batch 1 (2026-10-13)
