  // Knowledge Papers, book-style batch 2 (2026-10-13)
  "kp197-img-01.jpg":
    "Infographic titled Plant Nutrition Is a Living System, Not an Input Prescription: PQNK restores the natural ecosystem so plants can get what they need, when they need it. The plant builds its body mainly from air and water: about 95 to 97 percent of its dry matter comes from carbon dioxide and water captured through photosynthesis. About 4 percent is nitrogen from the air, brought into the system by nitrogen-fixing microorganisms in soil pores, on roots and in plant tissues. About 0.083 percent is the net mineral draw from the soil's geological reserve, already present in soil minerals and released in plant-available form by the continuous biological recycling of residues and organic matter. Nutrients are not missing; the living system that makes them available is what matters. Minerals in soil are not always available, because biology, roots and soil conditions decide their availability.",
  "kp195-img-01.jpg":
    "Infographic 1, The Plant Carbon Economy: the harvest is a dividend from a larger biological economy. Sunlight and atmospheric carbon dioxide enter the leaf through photosynthesis, and the captured carbon is shared three ways: reproductive output, the flowers, fruits, seeds, grain and tubers that humans harvest for food, feed and fibre; the above-ground structure of leaves, stems, buds, stored reserves and defensive compounds, part of which returns to the soil as fallen leaves and prunings; and the below-ground system of roots, root respiration, exudates, mycorrhizal partnerships and microbial life. Plants can transfer a substantial share of their carbon below ground, about 20 to 30 percent in wheat and barley in one study, and below-ground allocation is not the same as root exudation alone. Residues feed the soil life, which supports the next crop.",
  "kp192-img-01.jpg":
    "Infographic titled The Purchased-Input Treadmill: why agricultural production rises while farmer prosperity stalls. The problem: since 1970 a rising share of every rupee a farmer receives has gone on purchased seed, fertiliser, pesticides, irrigation, energy and machinery, reaching roughly 55 to 70 percent of receipts in many crops, while inflation-adjusted net farm income has stayed flat or declined. More inputs bring more costs and less income, and the system forces continuous spending just to stay in place. The solution: PQNK restores biological functions in six steps, restore the soil with no disturbance and permanent cover, manage water for a moist, aerated root zone with no flooding, encourage biodiversity, feed the soil rather than the plant, reduce purchased-input dependency, and keep more value with the producer.",
  "kp190-img-01.jpg":
    "Infographic titled Why Only 4 kg of NP During PQNK Transition? Not less nutrition, but the right nutrition at the right time in the right way: a small, well-managed dose supports the crop while the soil system that will feed it naturally is restored. The plant is built mostly from air and water: about 95.917 percent carbon, hydrogen and oxygen, about 4 percent nitrogen from the air, and about 0.083 percent from soil minerals. The soil holds the mineral reserve, and living biology converts it on demand into balanced, water-soluble nutrients that roots take up according to need, like wheat stored, milled into flour, made into dough and baked into bread only as it is eaten. Large one-time bags of fertiliser often go to waste through volatilisation, leaching, fixation and immobilisation before the crop can use them.",
